Since JSON is a subset of JavaScript, you can just use a script element, and including the json mime type certainly doesn&#x27;t hurt:<p><pre><code>    &lt;script type=&quot;application&#x2F;json&quot; id=&quot;myJSONData&quot;&gt;
      {
        &quot;name&quot;: &quot;John Doe&quot;,
        &quot;age&quot;: 30
      }
    &lt;&#x2F;script&gt;

    &lt;script&gt;
      const data = JSON.parse(document.getElementById(&#x27;myJSONData&#x27;).textContent);
      console.log(data.name + &quot; is &quot; + data.age); &#x2F;&#x2F; John Doe is 30
    &lt;&#x2F;script&gt;
</code></pre>
Note: The JSON data must exist literally within the script element. If you try using a src attribute to request it from elsewhere, the response will not be available via textContent or any other mechanism [0], and at that point you just need to fetch&#x2F;ajax&#x2F;xhr it.<p>[0] <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;stackoverflow.com&#x2F;questions&#x2F;17124713&#x2F;read-response-of-a-file-called-with-script-src-tag-from-external-sever" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;stackoverflow.com&#x2F;questions&#x2F;17124713&#x2F;read-response-o...</a>
